# the boom graphics facade: the Renderer, passes, and their resources
#
# a game authors shaders and draws through boom handles alone, never
# importing the backend libraries: Shader, Texture, Mesh, Material,
# RenderTarget, and the Renderer wrap mach-gl, mach-image, and mach-gltf
# state that stays hidden here, so swapping the GL backend for a Vulkan or
# SPIR-V one later touches no game code. texture_load decodes QOI and TGA
# images and mesh_load extracts glTF (.glb) geometry, both without
# exposing the backend types.
#
# the Renderer is the core abstraction (boom.graphics.renderer): a game
# creates one, then each frame calls renderer_begin_frame, runs one or
# more passes, and renderer_end_frame to present. a pass targets the
# window or an offscreen RenderTarget, carries a perspective camera (3D)
# or a pixel-space orthographic projection (2D), and submits mesh draws or
# sprite draws. custom shaders bind per pass or per material, so the game
# drives the pipeline. 2D and 3D go through the same machinery.
#
# a mesh is described by a VertexFormat (boom.graphics.vertex): a declared
# list of attributes rather than a fixed vertex struct. mesh_load builds
# the format from whatever attributes a glTF primitive provides, including
# the JOINTS_0 and WEIGHTS_0 attributes skinning needs.
#
# skeletal animation builds on that: model_load returns a Model (a skinned
# Mesh, a Skeleton, and named Animation clips) with mach-gltf fully hidden;
# model_player(name) gives an AnimationPlayer that pass_draw_skinned draws
# each frame. the runtime (pose sampling, joint composition, and the
# skinning palette) lives in boom.graphics.skeleton and
# boom.graphics.animation; boom.graphics.model is the loader.
#
# failures return Result[T, Error]; Error carries its message inline
# (boom.graphics.error), so no failure needs a heap allocation or a
# global. a live OpenGL context, which boom.window provides, is required.
# geometry math comes from boom.math.
#
# 3D mesh shader convention: attribute locations 0 a_position (vec3),
# 1 a_normal (vec3), 2 a_uv (vec2), and uniforms u_model, u_view,
# u_projection (mat4) that pass draws set each call; a material also sets
# u_base_color (vec4) and binds its textures to their sampler uniforms.
# consumers import this as `use boom.graphics;`.

